WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.318 --> 00:00:06.110
MARSH GARDINER: Hi.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.070
My name is Marsh.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.930
I'm a product manager
on the Apigee team.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.750
And joining me today
is Evan from HP.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.760
We'll be talking about
developer engagement

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.927
and how it supports
digital transformation.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.440
Digital transformation
is a less violent way

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.790
of saying software
is eating the world.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.240
It means adapting to business
opportunities presented

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.353
by digitally
enabled experiences.

00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:31.020
And I've spent the
last 10 years helping

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.330
Apigee customers adapt
their existing businesses

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.360
to this new landscape.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.958
While there are
tools that can help

00:00:37.958 --> 00:00:39.500
with some of this
transition, there's

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.320
also a cultural shift
that's happening.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.750
Digital transformation isn't
just a package install.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.200
It's a change in
how people work.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.180
Now, real, meaningful
change takes time,

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.730
but it also can
happen in bursts.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.680
For the last few months, as
the world's faced a pandemic,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.350
we've had to adapt faster.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.080
How we live, how we work,
it's dramatically different

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.900
than it was in January.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.650
To say we've been disrupted
would be an understatement.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.040
And now, today, I'm much
more likely to see my family

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.070
and friends through a
screen than in person.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.880
Adapting to shifts
and new technologies--

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.640
this has been going on
for a long, long time.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.040
Automation really got
going in the 1800s

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.410
as the power loom transformed
the textile industry

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.440
to programmable looms such as
the Jacquard machine, which

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.110
then made it easier
to reproduce patterns.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.640
This changed the way
people do their work.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.720
If you fast forward to today,
now many physical objects

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.810
have digital interfaces.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.530
I can ask the Google Assistant
to turn on my lights.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.560
I've added a device
to my garage door

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.710
so that it opens when I
pull into the driveway.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.510
APIs are how these things
talk to each other.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.290
When I order
groceries on my phone,

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.810
APIs fire off the different
systems that make that happen.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.120
Teaching all these systems
how to talk to each other

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.730
and how to enable these
digital experiences,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.240
that requires
people, developers.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.020
And today, Evan and
I will be talking

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.885
about how enabling
developers is a critical part

00:02:09.885 --> 00:02:11.510
of this digital
transformation journey.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.800
So creating this great developer
experience-- you build APIs--

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.770
these are products-- and
you bring them to market.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.750
And keep in mind, there are
many go-to-market strategies.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.800
You need to find the
one that works for you.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.890
You might begin by trying to
improve your internal business

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.330
efficiency.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.370
Or perhaps your
best opportunity is

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.040
to launch APIs that
support a partner program.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.470
Or maybe the
opportunity is creating

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:40.540
APIs for a large
number of developers

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.370
that you've never met.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:43.780
Each of these
approaches is going

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.520
to affect how you
think about what

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.860
will make a great developer
experience for each

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.310
of those channels.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.970
So first, developers
are your customers,

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.840
whether they're for internal or
external or partner use cases.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.020
They're the consumers
of your APIs.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.330
Second you, need a way
to bring them to market.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.590
That's a storefront.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.270
Just like any product
in a marketplace,

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:09.070
those APIs to be discovered,
learned about, and adopted.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.310
Third, we think of
a developer portal

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.090
as a storefront for APIs.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.260
This is where you are building a
great developer experience that

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.040
makes onboarding easy,
helps people get through

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.000
to their first API requests.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.178
And all the way
along that journey,

00:03:24.178 --> 00:03:26.220
when they have problems,
how do they get support?

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.780
Productizing your APIs,
that's one part of it.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.780
But in addition to being
valuable to developers,

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.565
your APIs have to
be discoverable.

00:03:37.565 --> 00:03:38.940
Once a developer
finds your APIs,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.040
they're going to need
great documentation.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.290
They're going to need support
and other resources that

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.922
help them succeed.

00:03:45.922 --> 00:03:48.130
You can't just go out and
buy digital transformation.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.420
It's part of how you
change your business.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.940
But there are tools that
can help you on your way.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.940
Just as important, there
are hard-fought lessons

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.482
you can learn from
companies who have

00:03:56.482 --> 00:03:59.603
been going through these
transformations, like HP.

00:03:59.603 --> 00:04:02.020
They've been on this journey
for five, six, or seven years

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.130
at this point.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.890
And with that, I'd
like to turn it over

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.040
to Evan who can talk to you
about how HP uses developer

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.170
experience to drive their
digital transformation

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.190
strategy.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:13.190
Evan, great to have you.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:13.870
Good morning.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.470
EVAN SCHEESSELE:
Thank you, Marsh.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.579
Yeah, really appreciate
the opportunity to speak--

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:20.680
Google's invitation,
and about this topic

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.250
that we both share so
much enthusiasm for.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.990
Myself, Evan
Scheessele, I've worked

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:27.880
at HP for about 20 years.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.900
And in my space is developer
engagement and API strategy

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.270
generally.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.120
It's part of our company's
work in digital transformation.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.140
In fact, I work in our Office
of Digital Transformation

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.440
and Transformation Organization.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.020
Today, I'm going to talk
about what this journey has

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.100
been like for us.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.090
And what's important is that
it's not a done journey.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:54.050
It's underway.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.780
There's a lot we've
been learning.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.030
And that means that there
have been course corrections

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:58.790
along the way.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.060
And that's an important
aspect that I really

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:02.740
want to have come through.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:04.900
Specifically, though,
there's a few points

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.720
I just want to make sure
that you are attending to

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:12.010
and that you take away by the
end of this brief session.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.500
Like Marsh said,
APIs aren't projects.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:15.920
They're products.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.340
And there's a rich
embodiment of that

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.370
that we've really
learned to embrace.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.320
API management is
essential, absolutely.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.580
But as an enabler of
this product lifecycle,

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:31.330
there's a marketplace in play
here that involves developers.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.510
And it might not
be what you think.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.610
And developer
experience is a thing.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:42.970
It's an important frame to
moving in a digital direction

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:44.980
and working with
APIs and developers

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:48.790
and creating that value that
enterprises seek to unlock.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.830
Now, this sounds like
an interesting story

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:51.910
coming from HP.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.580
And we're a hardware
company, right?

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.360
Back in the '30s we got
started, Bill Hewlett and Dave

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.410
Packard going to the
origins of Silicon Valley.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.420
In fact, the first
garage, if you've ever

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.840
heard of a startup in a garage,
the first Silicon Valley garage

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.390
were our founders
starting a company

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:12.910
and selling their first
product, that gray box

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.280
there, to Disney
to make Fantasia--

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.000
and constant adaptation
and adjustments

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.420
to their product
thinking along the way.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.890
What you see here
are, you know--

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.700
we're known for computers
and printers now.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.117
But on the screen,
you know, these

00:06:27.117 --> 00:06:29.200
are oscilloscopes and
calculators and our wrist

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.760
watch.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.340
It's a company that really
does embrace our motto,

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.600
"keep reinventing."

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.180
And that's been
essential to our movement

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.630
into digital and accelerating
in this direction

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:44.110
in the last 10 or so years.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.330
What we are today--

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:50.020
a lot of business-minded people
think about HP as the PC maker.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.050
And indeed, we sell more
PCs than any other company

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.010
on Earth.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:58.060
July 2020, just last month, we
held the number one position.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.190
And we obviously,
to most folks, know

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.080
that we have a huge
printing business,

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.390
from tiny little
handheld consumer

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:10.990
printers to presses that fill
factories, to 3D printing.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:13.270
That printing business
is essentially

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:17.020
based on this idea
of placing hardware

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:21.250
so that we can have a
supplies revenue stream, kind

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:25.670
of like the classic business
school razors and blades model,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:26.170
right?

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:27.628
You sell the Better
so that you can

00:07:27.628 --> 00:07:29.500
get that annuity
stream of revenue

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.760
from selling the blades.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.550
Or often, I think, also the
coffee maker and the coffee

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.000
pods is very similar.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.920
Well, with HP, you know,
we're known for ink,

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.970
placing printers so that
that ink sales happens.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.860
And what's great
about it, of course,

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:48.970
is we believe we do make
an important contribution

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.580
to society in this space,
because our content is

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:52.570
physical.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:56.740
HP makes, you know, the online
experience a little more real

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.720
when you can print a photo.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.960
And likewise, we're moving in
directions investing heavily

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:05.350
beyond just simple, you
know, classic printing.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:09.400
In some cases, we sell ink
by the 1,000 liter tote.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.920
And we sell materials
for 3D printings

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.840
in our nascent 3D
printing business

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.460
in the industrial side--

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.810
a lot of growth there in
this physical content space.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:22.220
But in a business
model point of view,

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:26.260
it's still about placing the
units and a lot of revenue

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.930
and a lot of business
happening through the use

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.500
and consumption of supplies.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.340
Now, all this is great.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.460
But this is a
software talk, right?

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.440
What does any of this
have to do with software?

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:38.898
Well, it's where
we're coming from.

00:08:38.898 --> 00:08:42.120
And it's an important
foundation for our direction

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.310
that we're heading now.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:48.210
Now, HP has had software
in mind for decades.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.430
We've even-- you know,
we've packaged software

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:51.300
with our devices.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.180
We've had to write device
drivers with operating system

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.950
partners.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.800
Often, though, software
is seen as an accessory

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.290
to be included in the box at the
lowest possible expense, just

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:07.260
a necessity that wasn't central
to the value proposition

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:11.820
of printers or PCs and such.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.950
It's got to evolve from there.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.400
Where we're coming from
is this old model, right,

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.680
this idea that there's a
factory and on the inside

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.520
we invent things.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.500
We make them then we
ship them out the door

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.250
to the outside world.

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.570
You know, marketing
brings it to market.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:29.940
We either have a direct or
indirect channel sales--

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:36.600
classic antiquated physical
hardware industrial model.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.430
So we're learning
to add software

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.880
as a principle to
this model, right?

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.680
Let's add another dimension.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.870
And keep that
hardware side, right?

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.510
We're still a mechanical
engineering, you know,

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.820
strong manufacturing company.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:57.340
But if we think of software as
its own piece to this equation,

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:00.750
now we can think of in the
factory or back office software

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.690
investments, which are essential
to a multinational company

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.710
like ours.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.720
But also, there's
the outside software,

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.070
like going to market
with software,

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:13.770
having digital businesses,
this cloud modern 2020

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:19.060
approach to building and growing
businesses based on software.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.610
So now we have a way
of thinking about it.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:22.830
Now, how do we make it real?

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.050
Well, we start with
our foundation.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.890
We have our hardware
placed all around the world

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:33.210
from small to large, consumer,
commercial, industrial.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:37.160
How do we grow these digital
businesses, these software

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:38.560
motions on top of it?

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.520
Well, it starts
with extensibility.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:47.700
How are we going to bring new
value through our hardware?

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.420
Well, this hardware has
to be software extensible.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.740
And that means
partnering, right?

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.950
Who's going to use
those extensions?

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.430
Who's going to
participate with us?

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.320
It's our business partners.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.063
Well, who among those
business partners?

00:11:00.063 --> 00:11:01.980
Well, those are the
developers, really, right?

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.020
We have to be working
with developers

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:07.980
to create these new experiences
to address those markets.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.390
And ultimately, what do
those developers need?

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:11.530
What do they consume?

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:13.600
It's our friend, the API.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:17.190
So this has been one of
our year's past epiphanies,

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:21.060
really getting our heads
wrapped around APIs aren't just

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.600
integration necessities,
like a new supplier needs

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.400
to be able to submit
an invoice and so they

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.250
have to call an API to
get paid or something.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:29.970
No.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.390
It's about growing ecosystems
and creating new value

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.000
in the business motion.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.580
So really, we've
recently gotten our hands

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.880
around the fundamental
value proposition of APIs.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.960
And so we're going to
really work with that.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.600
In fact, we started to adopt a
mindset of, build those APIs.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:51.960
Place those APIs.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.710
And they'll create value,
like that classic ink revenue

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.170
stream of placing
printers and seeing

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.970
their usage create revenue
through supplies, consumption.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:06.960
So maybe the API is the new ink.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.598
Well, let's manage that.

00:12:08.598 --> 00:12:10.890
If it's going to be that
fundamental and that critical,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.830
then we're going
to learn about APIs

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:17.100
and ensure we have structure
to manage and control

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.320
and really run them.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.640
So API management, right?

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.150
This is how we came to start
working with Apigee and Apigee

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:25.040
Google.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:30.990
And our work is this imperative
to ensure that we have a firm

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.690
grasp on our API strategy.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.400
Management is essential, right?

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.420
These assets are too important.

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.610
And so we did our research.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:44.130
We really came to find that
the Apigee's enterprise savvy

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:48.150
data model and structure
for federation and security,

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.130
authentication, a
lot of great features

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:54.600
that map to our kind of
enterprise scale complexity.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.080
Really important
work to get our arms

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:02.510
all wrapped around the
API management space.

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:05.970
And again, just classic back
office muscle that we had,

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.770
like, we need a new ERP.

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.680
We know how to bring
that in and bring it up

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.340
to speed to serve our business.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:14.910
API management--
same kind of motion.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:18.900
Research, adopt, build
out that capability.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:22.440
And you know, the outside
world tells us this

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.580
is an appropriate motion.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:27.720
Here's Gartner's view of
enterprise or digital business

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.130
landscape.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:33.570
Platform thinking, like a
robust API management suite

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.640
is a software platform, not
just a tool you use wire in.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.470
And where does it fit
on a full enterprise

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:42.690
view of this landscape?

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.360
Well, it's in that
partnering facet,

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.850
where API management
and the developer

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:51.990
portal components go, whether
it's customer facing or it's

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:53.810
internal or partner facing.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:55.915
Like, that that's the
side of this landscape

00:13:55.915 --> 00:13:56.790
that we're operating.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.760
And it was reassuring
to see that, you know,

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.510
enterprise business
strategists and deep thinkers

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:07.260
are putting this work as
a major facet of our go

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.160
forward investments.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.030
So for us, that meant
a lot of investment

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:15.990
in understanding the data model
and the controls, the knobs,

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:19.380
how do we structure our
thinking around API management.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.570
Obviously, Apigee is
a product foundation.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:24.540
But it's got this organizational
or data model structure

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.730
that, for a
non-trivial enterprise,

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.940
you have to wield that
appropriately and make

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:33.210
it fit into your
organizational landscape.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.690
And then deeper
in the data model,

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.050
there's all of these
other elements that

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:38.730
serve an important
purpose and allow

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:42.870
us to hone toward getting
the most value out

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:45.060
of the management platform,
the most visibility

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:49.570
and observability and security.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.660
These are essential pieces.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.760
But then what we've
learned, as we've

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:57.450
been doing this for the
last five years now,

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:02.000
we learned that this wasn't
necessarily the goal, right?

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.970
You know, rolling
this out and making

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:08.385
those in those
integrations work,

00:15:08.385 --> 00:15:10.260
rolling it out, making
the integrations work,

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.780
and ensuring the
controls are in place

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:18.330
was necessary, but wasn't
our final objective.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:19.890
Oh, something's missing there.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.010
And our developers, our
own internal developers,

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.480
kind of helped
remind us of this.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.660
And that is this idea that I
mentioned that, really, there's

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:31.230
a product management story.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.795
And if there's a product,
that means there's a customer.

00:15:34.795 --> 00:15:37.170
And if there's a customer,
there's a customer experience.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.060
So, you know, your
MBA types are going

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.640
to-- lightbulbs are going
off and saying, that's right.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:45.030
Customer experience matters,
no matter what back office

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.500
IT process or HR
process or anything.

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:50.650
There's always a relationship
and there's an experience.

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:53.970
And so we've recently
pivoted to started--

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:56.820
really internalized the
thinking that API management

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:02.100
is a foundation on which
we build a product value

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:09.330
proposition, and that the API is
the product, not just a managed

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:14.280
element of our model, of our
operations and integrations.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:16.230
So we've moved
API management now

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.100
as this foundational enabler.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:20.640
It's like this, you
know, well-built road

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.150
on which commerce can run.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:27.290
On that, now, we can build
software development standards

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.110
and process automation
pipelines that

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:34.430
will ensure consistent
software projects and services.

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:37.400
And then on that is the
real work of product

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:41.960
management, the full lifecycle
from birth to getting it out

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.910
in front of customers
to iterating

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.610
to even sunsetting products.

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.380
This is our new frame.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.170
The API isn't the
tip of that pyramid.

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:56.100
Really, it's the product that
has those relationships rolled

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:56.600
up in it.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.760
So it's about relationships.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:04.880
It's about relationships
with developers.

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:09.760
And that means that
this original work

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.819
of understanding the data
model remains in place.

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:17.859
But we've learned to
start shifting our focus

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:19.589
to another dimension, right?

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:21.819
It's going up in
the business model

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:24.910
stack into seeing
those relationships

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.520
and measuring the
engagement with the product.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.030
We're now up into even
net promoter score space.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:34.050
We're thinking about the product
and the value it's creating.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.790
This is a graph that
is fundamentally

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:38.810
a different view on the work.

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:41.140
This is about the relationships
between different kinds

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:45.580
of developers and the APIs
and the product embodiment

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:49.270
of those APIs, but
also the runtime--

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:51.790
real time nature of APIs.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:54.280
And we've even learned to
embrace that developers

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:55.400
come in different types.

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:57.380
There's the developers
who make your APIs

00:17:57.380 --> 00:17:59.620
and the developers who
consume those APIs.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:03.070
And they have different
kinds of relationships

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:06.220
and different stages
of life cycles.

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:08.920
Reliability and security
remain essential, right?

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:12.480
The runtime remains essential,
but our focus is now--

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:15.250
or maybe our sight
line has shifted

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:20.135
to better see how the value is
really created by this work.

00:18:20.135 --> 00:18:21.510
Now, some of you
might say, well,

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.310
isn't this developer relations?

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:24.670
We're talking marketing now?

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:28.850
And no, we're really thinking
still about the platform.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:31.900
But in a sense, yes, we're
enriching our platform thinking

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:35.980
with a concept of
relationships and developers'

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:41.950
experience with the product
as a first order concern.

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:44.600
There is a classic
value chain view

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.750
that might be familiar
to some of you.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:50.530
This is a reflection of
Apigee's original digital value

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:54.010
chain, where we've got
users who consume apps built

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:57.490
by developers who
consume the API that

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.710
is offered by these publishers.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:02.830
And what we're doing now and
how we're investing our energy

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:06.010
looking at this space
from 2020 going forward

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:09.640
is we're really looking at a
couple of different developer

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:10.370
journeys.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.220
There's the consumer
developer journey

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:15.580
and there's this publisher
side, the creator side.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:17.680
They're developer
experiences each,

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.060
but they're also different.

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:24.900
And you know, this
is a different step

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.760
or a different element
entirely of working with APIs

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:29.880
than thinking about
just publish APIs

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.260
and the value is
inherent, right?

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:35.340
We're no longer just
thinking about shipping APIs

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:37.500
like we'd ship a
printer hardware

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:42.480
and there would be
value inherent in that.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:46.680
We're thinking about the
actions of these consumers,

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:51.120
of these publishers, as
being the measured value

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:52.560
is happening there.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.140
In fact, there's a
gap in this diagram

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.550
that is the missing piece.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:19:59.640
And it's where they
find each other.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.550
And where's the
classic business school

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:06.080
concept of where suppliers
and consumers find each other?

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.190
Well, it's a marketplace.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.710
I use the word marketplace
very generally.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.200
We're always thinking in
terms of a developer portal,

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.340
more or less.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.430
But it's a marketplace
of ideas is

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:20.520
what I've learned to adopt
as a philosophy for why

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:23.400
the portal serves
the features it has

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:26.520
and how you measure its success.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:27.840
It isn't just uptime.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.460
It isn't just performance
of page views and such.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.980
It's, are consumers
finding what they're

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:40.530
looking for and unlocking
value from a catalog of assets

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:44.850
published by our
developer community?

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:46.860
If HP is going to
offer APIs, we need

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.550
to offer APIs that are
products which unlock value.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:53.610
And they only matter--
even the best API product

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.550
doesn't matter unless
it's consumed by somebody

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:01.900
who is having that
value, is experiencing it

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:03.950
and solving their problems.

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:07.570
So really, this puts in
perspective this portal work,

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:08.070
right?

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.520
Why does this portal
fit into this equation?

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:13.570
It's because it's where
these two communities can

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:16.450
meet each other in a
credible, believable--

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:19.980
it will be that
marketplace where

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:24.330
my participating community
finds what we're doing.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:28.530
Also, beyond the portal, is
just a place for discovery.

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.620
We're also learning
that it unlocks insight

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:33.750
through instrumentation.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.690
Analytics happens throughout
the digital value chain.

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:40.470
But at the portal where
products are being published

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:44.910
and iterated and our internal
and external partners are

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:48.270
finding them, evaluating them,
and choosing and engaging

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:51.180
with our APIs,
there's rich analytics

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:53.010
that come out of
that that better

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.610
inform how well this
platform is working

00:21:56.610 --> 00:22:01.020
and what further
investments are needed.

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:05.290
So looking forward,
simplifying our energy here,

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:08.490
we're putting energy into
that discovery and engagement

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:12.880
part-- the portal not just
as, again, a web platform,

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:16.110
but as a place for both the
consumers and the publishers

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:21.180
to efficiently and confidently
perform their work, right,

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:25.080
either searching for or finding
and getting on with life using

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:28.320
some API or SDK, but
also the publishers who

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.630
need to know that this
is how the catalog works.

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:32.220
This is the ultimate
catalog where

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:35.340
I want my work to be
discovered on, and as

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:39.600
efficiently and as consistent
and securely as possible.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.670
On the other side, we
think about the developers

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:43.170
in community.

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:46.620
We have to invest in
their resources for Q&amp;A

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:52.180
and consistent standards and
topic tagging between things

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:54.540
so that search works.

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:56.610
Good, solid answers are
always available when

00:22:56.610 --> 00:23:00.750
needed on how to run and
be a successful developer

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:02.070
in our company.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:05.040
And connecting it--
the interstitial fabric

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.360
is analytics.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:10.440
It's understanding
really what connects

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:11.520
all of these experiences.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.490
We can instrument a lot of
our digital tool changer--

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:19.830
developers' code pipelines
and API run times and even web

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:21.510
analytics on the portal--

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:24.930
together, creating a really rich
graph that better illuminates

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:29.430
how well we are
addressing our customers,

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:33.280
our developer partners' needs.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.630
So the takeaway
is, it's a journey.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:38.320
And it's not about
any one end goal,

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:41.260
but it is about being willing
to reinvent how you think

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:45.070
about your partners, think
about opening interfaces,

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:47.930
think about APIs as products.

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:50.590
There is a whole
lifecycle in APIs

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:55.050
that recently has come to light
for us on how to invest in it,

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:57.160
you know, from end to end.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.130
And API management--
absolutely essential.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:02.640
Apigee has been a
powerful enabler for us

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.680
to meet our needs
for management.

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:09.550
But now we're also seeing how
it further extends that value

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:12.020
into the product lifecycle.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.990
Developers need a
marketplace for their ideas,

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:16.490
whether you're
looking for something

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:20.630
or you're looking to ensure
your work is discoverable.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:24.230
And the best way to understand
those developers' needs

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:26.330
and requirements
and expectations

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:29.300
is what we call
developer experience.

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:31.970
It supports all of those
different developer journeys.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:34.190
And it's the right
kind of product frame

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:38.810
of mind for thinking about
unlocking your enterprise's

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:41.570
ultimate value as you
move from hardware

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:44.060
or whatever legacy business
structure to really

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.420
services that build on
top of your foundations

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:51.250
and help you move
forward and compete.

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:53.960
And with that, thank you
very much again, Marsh,

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:54.900
for the invite.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.500
And if you have any questions,
you can reach out to me--

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.312
evan@hp.com.

00:24:59.312 --> 00:25:00.520
MARSH GARDINER: Thanks, Evan.

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:03.160
It's really fascinating to hear
you talk through the journey HP

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:04.870
has been on and to
put it in the context

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:07.350
of the different
transformations that HP

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:11.440
has faced through years, like
the shift to delivering ink.

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:12.815
And it's really
great to hear how

00:25:12.815 --> 00:25:15.402
you've walked through that
and given lessons to us

00:25:15.402 --> 00:25:16.360
that we can learn from.

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:17.600
Thank you very much.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:19.142
EVAN SCHEESSELE:
You're very welcome.

00:25:19.142 --> 00:25:19.830
Thank you.

